LogTScript Documentation v0.3.2
Logic Values and Type Predicates
LogTScript logic supports several kinds of values that can participate in facts, rules, queries, and compound structures.
Type predicates allow rules and queries to test the type of the value currently bound to a variable.

───

Value types
The core logic value categories are:

Type
Example
Description

atom
john, boardwalk, red
Symbolic constant

number
15, -4, 250
Integer value

bool
0, 1
Boolean value

text
"John Smith"
Text value

list
[10, 20, 30]
Ordered collection

compound
person(john, 25)
Structured value with a functor and arguments


Variables are not values themselves. A variable becomes bound to one of the above values during resolution.

───

Type predicates
The following predicates test the type of the current value:
logts
atom(X)
number(X)
bool(X)
text(X)
list(X)
compound(X)

These predicates are type tests. They do not generate arbitrary values.
For example:
logts
value(10)
value("hello")
value(red)

query numbers:
    value(X),
    number(X)

The query produces:
text
X = 10

while:
logts
query textValues:
    value(X),
    text(X)

produces:
text
X = "hello"

and:
logts
query atoms:
    value(X),
    atom(X)

produces:
text
X = red


───

Type checking with bound variables
When the variable is already bound, the type predicate simply tests that value.
logts
value(42)

query valid:
    value(X),
    number(X)

X becomes 42, and:
text
number(42)

succeeds.
If the value is incompatible:
logts
value("42")

query valid:
    value(X),
    number(X)

the number(X) goal fails.

───

Unbound variables
A variable does not have to be bound when a relation starts.
For example:
logts
score(10)
score(20)
score(50)

validScore(X) <-
    score(X),
    number(X),
    X >= 0,
    X =< 100

query valid:
    validScore(X)

Resolution proceeds by binding X through score(X):
text
X = 10 → number(10) ✓
X = 20 → number(20) ✓
X = 50 → number(50) ✓

The query therefore produces three solutions.
A type predicate does not generate numbers:
logts
number(X)

does not mean "generate a number for X".
It means:
The current binding of X must be a number.
Therefore, goal order can matter:
logts
validScore(X) <-
    score(X),
    number(X),
    X >= 0,
    X =< 100

can work because score(X) binds X before the type test.

───

A variable may have different types across solutions
A logic variable does not have one permanent type across all backtracking solutions.
For example:
logts
value(10)
value("hello")
value(25)
value("world")

query values:
    value(X)

produces:
text
X = 10
X = "hello"
X = 25
X = "world"

The same variable X therefore has different types in different solutions.
A type predicate filters those solutions:
logts
query numericValues:
    value(X),
    number(X)

produces:
text
X = 10
X = 25

while:
logts
query textValues:
    value(X),
    text(X)

produces:
text
X = "hello"
X = "world"


───

Lists
A list is a distinct logic value representing an ordered collection.
Examples:
logts
[]
[1]
[1, 2, 3]
["John", "Mary", "Peter"]

A list can contain values of different types:
logts
[10, "hello", red, person(john, 25)]

The list itself is a list:
logts
list(X)

but its elements have their own types.
For example:
logts
value(["John", "Mary", "Peter"])

query names:
    value(X),
    list(X)

succeeds because X is a list.
However:
logts
text(X)

does not succeed.
A list containing text values is not itself a text value.
Conceptually:
text
X = ["John", "Mary", "Peter"]

list(X)  ✓
text(X)  ✗

To test the elements, use a relation that accesses the elements:
logts
member(Item, X),
text(Item)

This can produce:
text
Item = "John"
Item = "Mary"
Item = "Peter"


───

Compound values
A compound value is a structured value consisting of a functor and a fixed number of arguments.
Example:
logts
person(john, 25)

This is a compound value with:
text
functor = person
arity   = 2

Another example:
logts
rents(50, 200, 600, 1400, 1700)

has:
text
functor = rents
arity   = 5

The entire value is a single compound value.
logts
value(person(john, 25))

query structures:
    value(X),
    compound(X)

produces:
text
X = person(john, 25)


───

Compound values vs lists
Lists and compound values are distinct semantic types in LogTScript.
A compound has a named functor and fixed arity:
logts
person(john, 25)

A list is an ordered collection whose length can vary:
logts
[john, mary, peter]

Therefore:
text
compound(person(john, 25))  ✓
list(person(john, 25))      ✗

list([john, mary, peter])   ✓
compound([john, mary])      ✗

This distinction is intentional even though some Prolog implementations internally represent lists using compound terms.
LogTScript treats them as different logic value categories.

───

Destructuring compound values
Compound values can be matched directly through unification.
For example:
logts
property(
    boardwalk,
    400,
    [50, 200, 600, 1400, 1700],
    200
)

A query can destructure the compound:
logts
query propertyInfo:
    property(Name, Price, Rents, HotelPrice)

producing:
text
Name       = boardwalk
Price      = 400
Rents      = [50, 200, 600, 1400, 1700]
HotelPrice = 200

The list remains a single value bound to Rents.
It can subsequently be processed by list relations.

───

Lists inside compound values
Compound values and lists can be combined naturally.
For example, a Monopoly property can be represented as:
logts
property(
    boardwalk,
    400,
    [50, 200, 600, 1400, 1700],
    200
)

This remains property/4 regardless of how many rent values are stored in the list.
This avoids expanding the relation into something such as:
text
property/8
property/9
...

for every additional list element.
The number of rent values is instead represented by the list:
logts
[50, 200, 600, 1400, 1700]


───

Element type vs container type
The type of a container is independent from the type of its contents.
For:
logts
Names = ["John", "Mary", "Peter"]

the value has type:
text
list

while each element has type:
text
text

Therefore:
logts
list(Names)

succeeds, but:
logts
text(Names)

fails.
A relation operating on an element can test its type independently:
logts
member(Name, Names),
text(Name)

This distinction applies to all compound and collection values.

───

Type predicates are ordinary logic goals
Type predicates can be used anywhere a normal goal can be used:
logts
validScore(X) <-
    number(X),
    X >= 0,
    X =< 100

logts
validName(X) <-
    text(X)

logts
validProperty(X) <-
    compound(X)

logts
validRentTable(X) <-
    list(X)

They participate in normal conjunction and backtracking.
They do not introduce a separate type-checking language.

───

Example — mixed values
logts
inline [logic] .world:

    value(10)
    value("hello")
    value(red)
    value([1, 2, 3])
    value(person(john, 25))

    query numbers:
        value(X),
        number(X)

    query texts:
        value(X),
        text(X)

    query atoms:
        value(X),
        atom(X)

    query lists:
        value(X),
        list(X)

    query structures:
        value(X),
        compound(X)

:

The queries classify the same knowledge base without modifying it.

───

Design principle
Type predicates are filters, not generators.
logts
number(X)

does not generate arbitrary integers.
logts
text(X)

does not generate arbitrary strings.
logts
list(X)

does not generate arbitrary lists.
They test the current candidate binding produced by the rest of the logic program.
This preserves the normal LogTScript logic model:
text
facts/rules
    ↓
unification
    ↓
candidate bindings
    ↓
type predicates / comparisons / other goals
    ↓
solutions

And because the type is evaluated for each binding, the same variable can legitimately have different types across different backtracking solutions.
